    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d52">

                <pb facs="0143" n="105" sameAs="frus:frus1964-68v16/pg105"/>

                <head>52. Telegram From the Embassy in Greece to the
                        Embassy in Turkey<anchor n="1" xml:id="d52-f1"/>
                </head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>Athens</placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1964-06-04T21:30:00+02:00">June 4, 1964, 9:30 p.m.</date>
                    </dateline></opener>
                <p>336. Ref: Ankara’s 500.<anchor n="2" xml:id="d52-f2"/>
                </p>
                <list>
                    <label>1.</label>
                    <item>We have nothing “firm” from Athens to offer Erkin. Embtel 1812 (333 to Ankara)<anchor n="3"
                            xml:id="d52-f3"/> contains confidential report Tuomioja-Papandreou <pb facs="0144" n="106"
                            xml:id="pg106"/> meeting yesterday, which indicates Papandreou still
                        holding to line of enosis. We continue to believe there is no chance of
                        Greek support partition or federation of Cyprus, as apparently demanded by
                            Erkin, although there could
                        likely be guarantees for Turkish minority of Lausanne Treaty type. Other
                        possibilities are those suggested Embtels 1722 and 1759 (318 and 325 to
                            Ankara),<anchor n="4" xml:id="d52-f4"/> but even these cannot be put
                        forward as having Greek approval.</item>
                    <label>2.</label>
                    <item>Required action for moment seems to me is for Turks to tell Kucuk firmly
                        not to attempt establish de facto partition. I must emphasize that if Turks
                        move to intervene on island, Greeks will react promptly and in force. GOT should be left under no misapprehension on
                        this score.</item>
                </list>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        
                            <hi rendition="#bold">Labouisse</hi>
                        
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d52-f1" n="1" type="source" xml:id="d52-f1-content">
                <p>Source: Department of State, Central Files, POL 23–8 CYP. Secret; Flash. Repeated
                    to the Department of State, Nicosia, London, and Paris for USRO. Received in the Department of State at 3:44
                    p.m. and passed to the White House. The source text is the Department of State
                    copy.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d52-f2" n="2" xml:id="d52-f2-content">
                <p>Critic 2 from Ankara (<ref target="#d50">Document 50</ref>) was repeated to
                    Athens as telegram 500.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d52-f3" n="3" xml:id="d52-f3-content">
                <p>Dated June 4. (Department of State, Central Files, POL 23–8 CYP)</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d52-f4" n="4" xml:id="d52-f4-content">
                <p>Telegram 1722 is dated May 18. (Ibid.) Telegram 1759 has not been found.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
